<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Security - the Security service of the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition.

      Port        5104
      Transport   REST + OpenAPI (chosen so that consumers' stock JwtBearer handlers can fetch
                  /.well-known/jwks.json and the OIDC discovery document with zero bespoke code;
                  gRPC here would push hand-written JWKS retrieval into three other services)
      Role        SOLE JWT ISSUER for the whole system. Every other service holds verification
                  material only and never mints a token.
      Replaces    the legacy framework's cryptographic library, ten exported objects whose class
                  surface alone declares 65 public overloads, every one of which is reimplemented
                  on the Base Class Library - see the package section below. The library to
                  service assignment is recorded in docs/SERVICE_MAPPING.md.

    Deliberately minimal: this file carries ONLY what is specific to this project.

    Inherited from the repository-root Directory.Build.props and restated nowhere here (a second
    copy of a single decision is a defect - the two copies diverge):
      target framework, language version, nullable reference types, implicit usings,
      warnings-as-errors, the .NET analyzers, central package version management, deterministic
      build and the shared assembly metadata.

    Inherited from the repository-root Directory.Packages.props:
      every package version. Central Package Management is in force, so every PackageReference
      below is VERSIONLESS by requirement, not by omission.

    Conventions relied on rather than restated:
      * AssemblyName and RootNamespace default from this file's name to PowerFramework.Security,
        which is the assembly the service Dockerfile's ENTRYPOINT invokes. Do not rename either
        the file or the directory.
      * OutputType is not set: the Web SDK already produces an executable. (An explicit Exe is
        needed only by the sibling xunit.v3 test project, which is a different file.)
      * appsettings.json and appsettings.Development.json are picked up and copied to the output
        directory by the Web SDK's default globs, so they need no explicit item here.

    Deliberately absent, each for a stated reason:
      * No UserSecretsId. The signing secret reaches this service at run time from the
        orchestration secret layer through the options pattern only; a local secret store must
        not be implied as a delivery mechanism, and no credential of any kind belongs in a
        project file, an appsettings file or a container definition.
      * No container-tooling properties. The service Dockerfile and orchestration/docker-compose.yml
        own containerization, and they build from a repository-root context.
      * No GenerateDocumentationFile. With warnings-as-errors it would promote CS1591 across
        every public member, and nothing requires an XML documentation file: the OpenAPI
        description text is authored explicitly in the endpoint declarations.
      * No client-side, static-web-asset, SPA-proxy or Razor/Blazor property or item. Phase 1 is
        API and service level only: there is no presentation surface, no component library and
        no theming layer in scope, so no such property or item belongs here.
      * Nothing from the read-only PowerBuilder legacy tree is compiled, embedded or copied. That
        tree is the behavioural oracle: it is read as specification, never built.
  -->

  <ItemGroup Label="Project references - shared libraries only, never another service">
    <!--
      The published contract boundary. Carries OpenApi/security.v1.yaml, the authoritative wire
      document for this service's REST surface, as content so it travels with the build and can
      be asserted against.

      Note there is deliberately no security protocol-buffer definition and therefore no
      generated C# type for this surface: the Contracts project's .proto items cover the gRPC
      services only. Request and response types here are hand-authored beside the endpoints and
      conform to the YAML by review.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj" />
    <!--
      The ported kernel: the return-code algebra whose values the problem+json bodies carry, the
      30 CRYPTO_* constants (hash types, cipher types, cipher modes with their ECB default, RSA
      padding with its PKCS number 1 default, random-string and GUID flags, and the legal RSA key
      sizes), the tri-state IsSucceeded / IsFailed predicates, and the formatting helpers. The
      kernel itself declares no package and no project reference, so this edge is pure.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Package references - versionless: versions live in Directory.Packages.props">
    <!-- Generates the OpenAPI document that describes this service's REST surface. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" />
    <!--
      Direct on purpose, and mandatory. Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi pulls Microsoft.OpenApi
      2.0.0 transitively, which trips NuGet audit advisory NU1903 (high severity). Advancing to
      the 3.x line instead breaks the build: the 10.0.x OpenAPI source generator is compiled
      against the 2.x object model and the generated XML-comment support file then fails to
      compile with CS0200 on a read-only media-type example property. The centrally pinned
      2.11.0, the highest published 2.x, is the only value that is both non-vulnerable and
      compatible, and this direct reference is the mechanism that makes that pin win over the
      transitive one.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" />
    <!-- Inbound JWT validation, on /v1/ping and on the authenticated cryptographic surface. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" />
    <!--
      Token MINTING. This is the only project in the repository permitted to reference it,
      because this service is the sole issuer; the other three validate only. Keeping the
      reference here and nowhere else makes the sole-issuer topology visible in the build graph
      rather than merely documented.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" />
    <!--
      No cryptography package is referenced, deliberately. System.Security.Cryptography in the
      Base Class Library covers the whole legacy surface this service replaces: RSA key
      generation, encryption, decryption, signing and verification; keyed and unkeyed hashing
      over the MD5 and SHA family; DES, Triple DES and AES in ECB, CBC and CFB modes with
      PKCS-family padding; and cryptographically strong random generation. GUID generation comes
      from System.Guid. A third-party package would add supply-chain surface without adding a
      single operation.

      Nothing else belongs here either: no gRPC package, because this service is REST by
      decision; no HTTP resilience package, because this service has no outbound HTTP edge - its
      callers reach in, it does not reach out; no interactive OpenAPI UI package, because the
      document is produced already and a UI is not required; no third-party logging or telemetry
      package, because the built-in abstractions in the shared framework are the mandated stack;
      no validation framework; and no health-check package, because health-check registration
      ships in the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test support">
    <!--
      Grants the sibling test project access to the internal determinism seams: the randomness
      provider behind GenRandomBlob, GenRandomString and GenGUID, and the clock behind a token's
      issued-at and expiry claims. Both are the primary sources of non-determinism for
      characterization, and reaching them directly is what makes the per-service line-coverage
      gate attainable on the issuance path without resorting to reflection.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PowerFramework.Security.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
